software_isp: debayer_egl: Sample whole Bayer quads when downscaling

When the output is smaller than the input, the packed shader samples one
input pixel per output pixel and interpolates around it, so the colour
of an output pixel depends on which Bayer position it hits. The scale is
the output size over the native size (1920/3832 for a 1920x1080 stream
of a 3840x2640 sensor, 0.501), so that position drifts across the frame
and changes about every 240 output pixels in both directions. In a
dark, noisy picture each position gives a different cast once the black
level subtraction clips the noise, and the drift draws a chessboard of
coloured squares.

Below three quarters of the native size, take the red, blue and mean
green of the 2x2 quad the sample falls in instead, which treats every
output pixel the same way.

[sp11: seen on the Surface Pro 11's front camera in a dark room; reproduced with this shader under Mesa's llvmpipe
(flat dark noisy frame, 1920x1080 from 3840x2640: squares 4 levels apart, 0.6 with quads; a smooth scene renders
within one level of the interpolating path for RGGB and GRBG)]
---
 src/libcamera/shaders/bayer_1x_packed.frag | 44 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 src/libcamera/software_isp/debayer_egl.cpp |  7 ++++
 src/libcamera/software_isp/debayer_egl.h   |  1 +
 3 files changed, 52 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/libcamera/shaders/bayer_1x_packed.frag b/src/libcamera/shaders/bayer_1x_packed.frag
index 0b641a5f3..ffc15013f 100644
--- a/src/libcamera/shaders/bayer_1x_packed.frag
+++ b/src/libcamera/shaders/bayer_1x_packed.frag
@@ -70,6 +70,10 @@ uniform mat3 ccm;
 uniform vec3 blacklevel;
 uniform float gamma;
 uniform float contrastExp;
+/*
+ * 1.0 when the output is downscaled to three quarters or less, see quad_rgb().
+ */
+uniform float quad;
 
 float apply_contrast(float value)
 {
@@ -80,6 +84,43 @@ float apply_contrast(float value)
 		return 1.0 - 0.5 * pow((1.0 - value) / 0.5, contrastExp);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The colours of the 2x2 Bayer quad the pixel lies in: its red and blue pixels
+ * and the mean of its two green ones.
+ *
+ * When the output is downscaled, each output pixel samples one input pixel, and
+ * interpolating around it gives colours that depend on which Bayer position was
+ * hit. At a scale that is not exactly a half, that position drifts across the
+ * frame and changes every few hundred output pixels in both directions. In a
+ * dark, noisy picture each position gives a different cast once the black level
+ * clips the noise, so the drift draws a chessboard of coloured squares. Taking
+ * the whole quad treats every output pixel the same way.
+ */
+vec3 quad_rgb(vec2 pixel)
+{
+	vec2 q = floor(pixel * 0.5) * 2.0;
+
+	/* The quad's two columns are adjacent bytes of one packed group. */
+	float x = floor(BPP * q.x + 0.02);
+	float p00 = texture2D(tex_y, vec2(x, q.y) * tex_step).r;
+	float p10 = texture2D(tex_y, vec2(x + 1.0, q.y) * tex_step).r;
+	float p01 = texture2D(tex_y, vec2(x, q.y + 1.0) * tex_step).r;
+	float p11 = texture2D(tex_y, vec2(x + 1.0, q.y + 1.0) * tex_step).r;
+
+	/*
+	 * tex_bayer_first_red is 0.0 or 1.0 in each direction: red at (fx, fy),
+	 * blue at (1 - fx, 1 - fy), green at the other two positions.
+	 */
+	float fx = tex_bayer_first_red.x;
+	float fy = tex_bayer_first_red.y;
+	float r = mix(mix(p00, p10, fx), mix(p01, p11, fx), fy);
+	float b = mix(mix(p11, p01, fx), mix(p10, p00, fx), fy);
+	float g = (mix(mix(p10, p00, fx), mix(p11, p01, fx), fy) +
+		   mix(mix(p01, p11, fx), mix(p00, p10, fx), fy)) * 0.5;
+
+	return vec3(r, g, b);
+}
+
 void main(void)
 {
 	vec3 rgb;
@@ -226,6 +267,9 @@ void main(void)
 			vec3(patterns.y, C, patterns.x) :
 			vec3(patterns.wz, C));
 
+	if (quad > 0.5)
+		rgb = quad_rgb(center_pixel);
+
 	/*
 	 * \todo: Black level normalising, AWB and digital gain could be
 	 * reworked into a single multiplication.
